.TH TAIL 1
.SH NAME
tail \- deliver the last part of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.if t [ \(+-number[\fBlbcr\fR] ]
.if n +_number[lbcr]
[ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tail
copies the named file to the standard output beginning
at a designated place.
If no file is named, the standard input is used.
.PP
Copying begins at distance
.I +number
from the beginning, or
.I \-number
from the end of the input.
.I Number
is counted in units of lines, blocks or bytes,
according to the appended option
.BR l ,
.B b
or
.BR c .
When no units are specified, counting is by lines.
.PP
The option
.B r
copies lines from the end of the file in reverse order;
the default for
.B r
is to copy the whole buffer.
.SH "A BLOCK IS 3072 BYTES"
.I Note:
.B b
counts filesystem blocks, which are 3072 bytes here,
where v7 counted 512-byte PDP\-11 disk blocks.
So
.B tail \-1b
delivers the last 3072 bytes and
.B dd bs=1b
moves the same amount \- the two commands agree, which they would not
if this one had kept a number that names nothing on this machine.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dd(1)
.SH BUGS
Tails relative to the end of the file
are treasured up in a buffer of 4096 bytes, and thus
are limited in length.
.PP
Various kinds of anomalous behavior may happen
with character special files.
